# ASI OS — product context

ASI OS is a **private AI workspace** for one owner and a few invited friends. It is
being revamped from a manual system of record into a workspace in the shape of
[Odysseus](https://github.com/pewdiepie-archdaemon/odysseus), built on the security
spine this repository already has.

**The plan of record is `docs/ODYSSEY-BLUEPRINT.md`. Read it before proposing any
change.** Where it disagrees with `docs/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`, the blueprint wins;
where it disagrees with the code, the code wins and the blueprint is out of date.

## The thesis, in one sentence

Chat is the front door, but **the record is the product**: a conversation captures
thoughts, creates projects, sets next actions and proposes memory, and every one of
those writes lands in the same RLS-protected tables the manual surfaces read from,
with the same append-only audit trail.

## Rules that survive the revamp, and get more work to do

1. **Never fabricate.** No placeholder numbers, no sample data, no metric not read
   from a record. Every surface renders exactly one state from
   `components/os/states.tsx`. There is deliberately no component for a fake value.
   Applied to model output: a claim whose evidence does not resolve to a row the
   caller owns is **not shown**.
2. **The human keeps control, and the server decides what that means.** A
   server-side tool registry (`lib/tools/registry.ts`) determines which tools need
   approval. A model's own `requiresApproval` claim is advisory and ignored. A tool
   not in the registry has no implementation to reach.
3. **RLS is the only isolation boundary.** Application filters are defence in depth,
   never the mechanism. The app holds no privileged credential.
4. **Memory is visible, sourced, editable and forgettable.** Suggested memory is
   never injected into a prompt.
5. **Progressive disclosure.** One line by default, reasoning on request. Never open
   with a full trace.
6. **Structure only when real use earns it.** No vector DB, queue, worker, realtime
   layer or cron until something measurably needs one, with the evidence named.
7. **Calm by default; colour carries meaning.** Blue is the system thinking, amber
   means something needs you, sage means confirmed, red means failed. Nothing is
   coloured for decoration.
8. **Wired or absent.** A control that looks real and changes nothing is a trust bug,
   worse than a missing feature.

## What is deliberately NOT being built

Shell or code execution of any kind (Odysseus can afford it running on one person's
own machine; a hosted app three people share cannot). Local model serving and the
Cookbook — there is no GPU in this deployment. Deep Research, Documents, Compare,
MCP, image generation, email and calendar are Phase G and each is gated on the core
being good first. Do not build any of them early, and do not build an empty shell
for one.

## Phase discipline

`docs/ODYSSEY-BLUEPRINT.md` §8 defines phases A through G. **Do not implement a
later phase than the one being worked on.** If a task seems to require it, say so
and stop rather than reaching forward.
